.TH NIXALARM 5 "2026-11-20" "nixalarm 0.2.1" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
nixalarm \- configuration file for nixalarm
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nixalarm
reads a small TOML-like configuration file.
Command line options in
.BR nixalarm (1)
override the matching values for the current run.
A later assignment in the file overrides an earlier one.
Unknown keys are ignored.
Numeric conversions that fail keep the previous value and produce a warning on
standard error, as do the explicitly validated
.BR theme ,
.BR dial_time ,
.B analog_midnight_label
and
.B modulation
values described below.
Other invalid values are silent: anything but
.BR true ,
.BR 1 ,
.B yes
and
.B on
disables a boolean, an invalid color keeps the previous color, a list value
without brackets parses as empty, and an unknown source type parses as
.BR generated .
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
The default path is:
.PP
.RS
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nixalarm/config.toml
.RE
.PP
If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or empty, the fallback path is:
.PP
.RS
.I $HOME/.config/nixalarm/config.toml
.RE
.PP
If both variables are unset or empty, the relative path
.I config.toml
is used.
A missing file is normal for a read-only probe.
On a normal launch, a missing file is seeded with the defaults and then
loaded.
A directory in place of the file produces a warning and the built-in defaults
are used.
An existing file that cannot be read also produces a warning and the built-in
defaults are used.
.PP
The
.B --configure-source
wizard in
.BR nixalarm (1)
never seeds or modifies the file.
It only reads the named path, which must be a readable regular file or a file
that does not yet exist.
.SH VALUE SYNTAX
The parser supports simple
.I key = value
assignments, quoted strings, integers, floating point numbers, booleans, and
sections.
It is intentionally small and does not implement the full TOML language.
.PP
A
.B #
character starts a comment outside a quoted value.
Quoted values keep their own
.B #
characters.
A quote opens string mode only where a value can start: after
.BR = ,
.BR [ ,
or
.BR , .
An apostrophe elsewhere, as in an unquoted path, does not start a string.
.PP
Booleans accept
.BR true ,
.BR 1 ,
.B yes
and
.B on
to enable.
Any other value disables, without warning.
The
.B direct_sampling
source key is narrower: only the literal value
.B true
enables it.
.PP
Colors are
.B #rrggbb
hexadecimal strings.
An invalid color keeps its previous value, without warning.
.PP
The
.B alarms
list is a bracketed, comma-separated list.
Elements may be quoted individually.
A value without surrounding brackets parses as an empty list.
.PP
Section headers are
.BR [window] ,
.BR [style] ,
and
.B [sources.NAME]
where
.I NAME
is literal text: the reader does not unquote section headers, so names
containing spaces or dots work in the file itself.
The
.B --configure-source
wizard in
.BR nixalarm (1)
is stricter: it accepts only ASCII letters, digits,
.B _
and
.B -
and rejects anything else.
A second
.B [sources.NAME]
header resets that source to its defaults; never append a duplicate header to
override a built-in preset.
Edit the existing section instead.
.SH TOP-LEVEL KEYS
.SS Alarm schedule
.TP
.B alarm_source = "generated"
Name of the alarm source.
Overridden by
.B --source
and
.B --test-source
in
.BR nixalarm (1).
.TP
.B alarms = ["07:30"]
Default alarm times used when
.BR nixalarm (1)
is launched without a command line time.
Entries use the time format in
.BR nixalarm (1);
empty strings are skipped.
A single entry that does not parse discards the entire list, so any non-empty
list containing an invalid entry fails startup, as does a non-empty list of
only empty strings.
Use an empty list for clock-only launches; each alarm fires once and does not
recur.
See also
.I docs/users/alarms.md
in the repository.
.SS Playback
.TP
.B volume = 0.9
Playback volume, clamped to the range 0.0 through 1.0.
Applies to generated, decoded, MIDI, and SDR audio alike.
.TP
.B source_start_timeout_seconds = 8
Timeout in seconds, with a minimum of 1.
For file and network sources it sets the FFmpeg protocol input/output
timeout.
For RTL-SDR sources it bounds the wait for the first audio sample.
When a source yields no audio within the applicable wait, playback falls back
to
.BR fallback_source .
.TP
.B fallback_source = "generated"
Source tried once when the selected source yields no audio.
A source that played audio and then stopped does not fall back.
If the fallback also yields no audio, there is no further fallback.
An unknown fallback name plays the generated tone instead.
A generated primary source never fails, so it never falls back.
.TP
.B snooze_minutes = 10
Minutes added when Space is tapped while the alarm is ringing.
Values below 1 behave as 1.
.TP
.B hold_to_stop_seconds = 10
Seconds Space must be held continuously while ringing to dismiss the alarm.
Values below 1 behave as 1.
.SS Clock display
.TP
.B use_24_hour = false
Use a 24-hour clock display.
The default
.B false
uses a 12-hour display.
This key changes the seven-segment digits and the nixie tubes only.
The analog face keeps its 12-hour dial, and the dial themes follow
.BR dial_time ;
see
.B STYLE SECTION
below.
.SS Observer location
.TP
.B latitude = 0.0
Observer latitude in signed degrees, positive north, clamped to -90 through
90.
.TP
.B longitude = 0.0
Observer longitude in signed degrees, positive east, clamped to -180 through
180.
.PP
Both keys are used only by the
.BR sundial ,
.B moondial
and
.B auto_dial
themes, whose accuracy depends on them.
The default 0.0, 0.0 is a real location on the equator at the prime meridian,
not a sentinel; dial accuracy requires the true observer coordinates.
.SH WINDOW SECTION
.SS [window]
.TP
.B width = 800
Initial window width, with a minimum of 240.
.TP
.B height = 360
Initial window height, with a minimum of 160.
.TP
.B fullscreen = false
Start in fullscreen desktop mode.
Overridden by
.B --fullscreen
and
.B --windowed
in
.BR nixalarm (1).
.TP
.B always_on_top = false
Request an always-on-top window.
.TP
.B flash_hz = 2.0
Flash rate in hertz while the alarm is ringing.
The seven-segment face blanks on the off phase and the nixie face darkens its
glow.
Set to
.B 0
to disable flashing.
The analog and dial faces do not flash; a ringing dial is signaled by sound.
.SH STYLE SECTION
.SS [style]
.TP
.B theme = "terminal_glow"
Select the built-in visual theme.
A theme sets both the clock rendering style and its colors.
Set this before any color overrides, since selecting a theme resets the
palette and a later assignment in the file wins.
An unknown theme name produces a warning and keeps the previous theme.
See also
.I docs/users/appearance.md
in the repository.
.PP
Supported themes:
.RS
.TP
.B terminal_glow
Seven-segment digits: dark green background, bright green digits, dim inactive
segments, and glow.
.TP
.B sinnoh_green
Seven-segment digits in a light green pixel-clock style: light green
background, dark green block digits, no glow.
.TP
.B nixie
Photoreal nixie-tube digits rendered from bundled artwork.
Ignores the color keys below.
The
.B show_seconds
key selects the 6-tube HH:MM:SS layout when true and the 4-tube HH:MM layout
when false.
.TP
.B analog
Analog dial rendered from bundled SVG artwork with the hand motion driven in
code.
A small alarm marker appears when an alarm or snooze is pending and pulses
while ringing.
The face keeps its 12-hour dial regardless of
.BR use_24_hour .
The
.B show_seconds
key draws or hides only the seconds hand; the theme enables it by default.
The
.B roman_numerals
key selects the Roman-numeral face variant.
.TP
.B sundial
A horizontal sundial, ray-traced in OpenGL, telling time by the shadow the sun
casts at the configured
.B latitude
and
.BR longitude .
The plate tilt and hour spacing are optimized for that latitude, and the dial
is laid out for that latitude's summer solstice so it carries every hour its
light can ever be up for.
Hour bands are read by shadow position: the engraved lines are the boundaries
between hours, drawn at the half hours, with each hour's Roman numeral in its
own band.
Each boundary is a ray from the gnomon foot run out to the edge of the disc;
every hour line lies in a plane containing the style, so all of them converge
where the style meets the plate.
A dial reads apparent solar time, which is not clock time: solar noon can be
an hour or more from 12:00 depending on position within the time zone, the
equation of time, and daylight saving.
Requires OpenGL 3.3.
Uses only the
.B background
key below, which fills the area around the dial.
Dark once the sun can no longer be read.
.TP
.B moondial
The same instrument built for the Moon instead.
It is always rendered under the current Moon position, day or night.
Same requirements and color behavior as
.BR sundial .
.TP
.B auto_dial
Both dials as one clock.
Each dial is always rendered under its own light; the visible dial is the one
that can currently be read, crossfading from one to the other at the
changeover.
The changeover follows readability rather than sunset: a dial stops telling
the time once the gnomon's shadow slides off the edge of the plate, some while
before the body sets, and the moon dial takes over only if the Moon is up and
more than roughly a quarter lit, since a thinner crescent casts no readable
shadow.
When neither dial can be read the face holds the showing dial, rendered under
its current light.
Same requirements and color behavior as
.BR sundial .
.RE
.TP
.B background = "#07180b"
Window background color.
Honored by the seven-segment, analog, and dial themes.
Ignored by the
.B nixie
theme, which renders its own backdrop.
.TP
.B segment_on = "#6cff57"
Active segment color for the seven-segment themes.
The
.B analog
theme uses it only for the armed alarm marker.
Ignored by the
.B nixie
and dial themes.
.TP
.B segment_off = "#123319"
Inactive segment color for the seven-segment themes.
Ignored by the
.B nixie
theme, the
.B analog
theme, and the dial themes.
.TP
.B glow = true
Draw a soft glow behind active segments in the seven-segment themes.
Ignored by every other theme.
.TP
.B show_seconds = false
Show seconds in the clock display.
The seven-segment themes add seconds digits.
For the
.B analog
theme, this draws or hides only the seconds hand.
For the
.B nixie
theme, true selects the 6-tube HH:MM:SS layout and false selects the 4-tube
HH:MM layout.
The dial themes ignore this key.
.TP
.B roman_numerals = false
For the
.B analog
theme, use the Roman-numeral SVG face instead of the default Arabic-numeral
SVG face.
Ignored by every other theme.
.TP
.B dial_time = "apparent"
What the dial themes read.
.B apparent
is each dial body's own uncorrected time, as the real instrument tells it:
the sundial reads apparent solar time and the moondial reads apparent lunar
time.
.B mean
applies the equation of time to give local mean solar time for both dials;
it is derived from the sun even for the moon dial, so it also absorbs the
moon's daily retardation.
It is still keyed
to the configured longitude, not to the time zone's standard meridian, so it
does not agree with a watch.
.B clock
carries that the rest of the way to civil time, taking the zone offset and
daylight saving from the system, and is the setting that matches a watch.
The correction is applied by rendering the light at the hour angle a sun
keeping that time would have; declination is untouched, so shadows keep their
real seasonal length, and in a corrected mode the rendered light also rises
and sets at corrected times.
An invalid value produces a warning and keeps the previous mode.
Ignored by every other theme.
.TP
.B analog_midnight_label = "24"
Reserved for non-SVG 24-hour analog faces.
Ignored by the current
.B analog
theme, which follows its bundled 12-hour Arabic dial.
An invalid value produces a warning and keeps the previous label; only
.B 24
and
.B 0
are accepted.
.SH SOURCE SECTIONS
.SS [sources.NAME]
Each source section adds or overrides one audio source named
.IR NAME .
.PP
Supported source types are
.BR generated ,
.BR file ,
.BR internet ,
.BR midi ,
and
.BR sdr .
The legacy spelling
.B sdr_weatherband
is accepted as an alias for
.BR sdr .
An unknown type spelling parses as
.B generated
without warning.
Source selection and setup are also covered in
.I docs/users/sources.md
in the repository.
.SS File, stream, and MIDI keys
.TP
.B path = ""
Audio file for
.B file
and
.B midi
sources.
An empty path yields no audio, so playback falls back.
.TP
.B url = ""
Stream address for
.B internet
sources.
An empty address yields no audio, so playback falls back.
.TP
.B soundfont = ""
SoundFont file for
.B midi
sources.
If omitted or empty,
.B nixalarm
tries a short list of common system SoundFont paths.
If no SoundFont can be found, the source yields no audio, so playback falls
back.
.SS RTL-SDR source keys
.TP
.B device_index = 0
RTL-SDR dongle index.
.TP
.B frequency_mhz = 162.450
Target frequency in megahertz.
User sections without an explicit frequency keep this default.
.TP
.B modulation = "nbfm"
Demodulator:
.B nbfm
for narrowband FM, which covers the weather band and other public-service
channels and is the default;
.B wbfm
for wideband FM, meaning ordinary broadcast radio; and
.B am
for the broadcast and shortwave bands.
An invalid value produces a warning and keeps the previous value.
.TP
.B direct_sampling = false
Enable the tuner direct-sampling branch.
A bare RTL-SDR needs this branch to reach anything below roughly 24 MHz, the
AM broadcast band included.
An upconverter does the same job in hardware.
.TP
.B gain
Manual tuner gain in dB for
.B sdr
sources.
It has no default; a section without it is rejected when that source is used.
Quoted numeric values are accepted.
The value must be finite: empty values, the word
.B auto
in any letter case, non-numeric text, trailing characters, and infinities are
rejected.
The value is scaled by ten and truncated toward zero to whole tenths, matching
.BR rtl_fm ;
fractional digits beyond the tenths place are discarded, so 20.05 becomes
20.0.
Any value that truncates onto -100 tenths, including -10.0 itself, is rejected
because
.B rtl_fm
reserves that value for automatic gain.
Explicit zero is valid.
Validation rejects only values outside the signed integer tenths range the
backend stores; it does not judge reception quality, and there is no universal
recommended setting.
Gain controls the receiver, independently of playback
.BR volume .
.SS Generated source
.EX
[sources.beep]
type = "generated"
.EE
.SS Local file source
.EX
[sources.local_song]
type = "file"
path = "/home/user/Music/alarm.flac"
.EE
.PP
Files are decoded through FFmpeg.
Format support depends on the system FFmpeg build.
.SS Internet source
.EX
[sources.weather_stream]
type = "internet"
# Set url to a personal stream address.
url = ""
.EE
.PP
Network streams are decoded through FFmpeg.
A stream that yields no audio falls back to
.BR fallback_source .
.SS MIDI source
.EX
[sources.midi_alarm]
type = "midi"
path = "/home/user/Music/alarm.mid"
soundfont = "/usr/share/soundfonts/default.sf2"
.EE
.PP
MIDI files are rendered through FluidSynth.
If
.B soundfont
is omitted,
.B nixalarm
tries a short list of common system SoundFont paths.
If no SoundFont can be found, the source yields no audio and the configured
fallback source is used.
.SS RTL-SDR source
.EX
[sources.weather_sdr]
type = "sdr"
modulation = "nbfm"
frequency_mhz = 162.400
device_index = 0
gain = 20.0
.EE
.PP
The frequency and gain above are illustrative, not recommendations.
.PP
Required gain is checked for the selected SDR source and any reachable SDR
fallback before an alarm is armed or a source test starts.
Unused SDR sources, source listing, and clock-only operation require no gain
setup.
The offline default remains
.BR generated .
.PP
Run
.B nixalarm --configure-source NAME
for read-only setup instructions; see
.BR nixalarm (1)
for the wizard terminal requirements and exit status.
The wizard prints a gain assignment for an existing user section, or a
complete section for a built-in preset.
Source sections replace built-in definitions, so a gain-only section is not a
valid way to override a preset: add or replace the assignment in the existing
section without appending a duplicate section header.
.PP
SDR sources require RTL-SDR hardware and the
.BR rtl_fm (1)
program at runtime.
Missing hardware, a missing
.B rtl_fm
binary, and a source that yields no audio are runtime events, not startup
errors: the
normal fallback path plays
.B fallback_source
instead.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nixalarm/config.toml
Primary configuration file.
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/nixalarm/config.toml
Fallback configuration file when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or empty.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nixalarm (1),
.BR rtl_fm (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1)
